📱 STM32 + SIM800C SMS-Based LED Control System

An embedded systems project that uses an STM32 microcontroller and a SIM800C GSM/GPRS module to control an LED remotely through SMS messages. The system checks the sender's phone number before executing a command and automatically replies to unauthorized senders with a registration warning.

🚀 Project Overview

This project demonstrates how to integrate a GSM module with an STM32 microcontroller using UART communication and AT commands.

The SIM800C receives SMS messages and notifies the STM32 when a new message arrives. The microcontroller reads the message, extracts the sender's phone number, and checks whether the sender is authorized.

✅ Authorized user: Can turn the controlled LED ON or OFF using SMS commands.

🚫 Unauthorized user: Cannot control the LED and receives an SMS stating that they are not registered.

💡 LED debugging: LEDs connected to GPIO pins PB12, PB13, and PB14 indicate important stages of the communication and message-processing workflow.

📩 Automatic response: The system sends a READY SMS during startup and an authorization warning to unregistered senders.

✨ Features

📡 GSM communication using the SIM800C module

🔌 UART communication between the STM32 and SIM800C

📥 Incoming SMS notification using AT+CNMI

📖 SMS retrieval using AT+CMGR

🔐 Sender phone-number verification

💡 Remote LED control using on and off SMS commands

🚫 Unauthorized sender rejection

📤 Automatic SMS responses using AT+CMGS

🔍 GPIO-based debugging to monitor program execution

⚙️ How It Works:

The program follows a sequence of initialization, notification, message retrieval, authorization, and execution.

Initialize the GSM module: The STM32 sends AT commands and checks the responses.

Configure SMS operation: The module is configured for text-mode SMS and new-message notifications.

Send a startup message: The system attempts to send a READY SMS to the configured phone number.

Detect an incoming SMS: The STM32 looks for a +CMTI: notification containing the SMS storage index.

Retrieve the message: The firmware sends AT+CMGR to read the SMS and extract the sender's phone number and message body.

Verify authorization: The sender's number is compared with the phone number configured in the firmware.

Execute an authorized command: The SMS body is checked for on or off, and PB15 is set HIGH or LOW accordingly.

Reject unauthorized senders: If a sender's number is present but does not match the configured number, the firmware attempts to reply:

SORRY ,YOU ARE NOT REGISTERED!

🐛 Debugging with LEDs

The diagnostic LEDs provide a simple way to observe the program without requiring a debugger or serial console.

PB12: Observe AT communication initialization and incoming SMS notifications.

PB13: Observe text-mode initialization and SMS retrieval.

PB14: Observe SMS notification configuration and unauthorized-response retries.

PB15: Observe the remotely controlled output.

This approach is useful for embedded debugging when UART output is occupied by the GSM module.
